{"event_id": "d26b01fe1ace", "event_type": "system_response", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T03:02:31.357262+00:00", "data": {"text": "T1078 Valid Accounts is a technique in the MITRE ATT&CK framework, and it refers to the exploitation of valid accounts on a system to gain unauthorized access. As a Linux system administrator, I can tell you that this technique can be devastating if not properly managed.\n\nTo give you a better idea, a valid account refers to a username and password combination that is allowed to access a system, either locally or remotely, via services like SSH (Secure Shell). These accounts can be legitimate users, administrators, or even service accounts. \n\nOn my system, for instance, I have a few users like `admin`, `user`, and `www-data` that have their own home directories in `/home`: `/home/admin`, `/home/user`, and `/home/www-data`. The `admin` user is allowed to access via SSH on port 22, while the `www-data` user is used by the `nginx` service, which runs on port 80 and 443 for HTTP and HTTPS respectively. \n\nThe `ssh` service is running on my system with the configuration in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config` where the `PermitRootLogin` is set to `no` to prevent root login via SSH. However, a valid account with a strong password", "persona_used": "novice"}, "source": "harness"}
